members.extended:
Scalars become shape `(1, 1, 1)`, 1-D tensors become `(1, N, 1)`,
2-D tensors gain a trailing unit axis to become `(M, N, 1)`;
tensors with rank $\geq 3$ pass through unchanged.

notes:
Mathematical definition:



$$
\text{fmax}(a_i, b_i) =
\begin{cases}
    b_i,           & a_i = \text{NaN}, \\
    a_i,           & b_i = \text{NaN}, \\
    \max(a_i, b_i),& \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

Contrast with `lucid.maximum`, which propagates NaN per IEEE 754.

Mathematical definition:



$$
\text{fmin}(a_i, b_i) =
\begin{cases}
    b_i,           & a_i = \text{NaN}, \\
    a_i,           & b_i = \text{NaN}, \\
    \min(a_i, b_i),& \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

Contrast with `lucid.minimum`, which propagates NaN per IEEE 754.

parameters.description:
If `True`, assumes both `elements` and `test_elements`
contain unique items. Accepted for reference-framework parity;
defaults to `False`.

notes:
Mathematical definition:



$$
\text{signbit}(x_i) =
\begin{cases}
    \text{True},  & \text{sign bit of } x_i \text{ is } 1, \\
    \text{False}, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

Non-differentiable: the gradient is zero almost everywhere and the
output dtype is boolean.

members.extended:
NumPy-style vertical split: cuts the input into pieces along axis 0.
With an integer `k`, the tensor is divided into `k` equal pieces;
with a sequence of indices, the cuts occur at those positions.
